::: {.doc-subject}

Calculate the Wiener index of the graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L84762-L84845){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.wiener_index(weighted=None)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Wiener index](../assets/graphs/wiener-index.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A six-node graph and a card that groups its 15 node pairs by shortest-path distance: 6 pairs at distance 1, 6 at 2, 2 at 3, and 1 at 4. Weighting each group by its distance sums to the Wiener index 28.0." width="100%"}

The Wiener index is the sum of all pairwise shortest path distances in the graph. It is a
fundamental topological descriptor used in chemistry and network analysis to measure graph
compactness.

For undirected graphs: `W(G) = Σ_{u < v} d(u, v)`. For directed graphs:
`W(G) = Σ_{u ≠ v} d(u, v)`.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True`, edge weights (`1` where missing) are the distances. If `False`, every edge
    has distance `1`. If `None` (default), follows whether the graph was created as
    weighted.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The Wiener index. Returns `0.0` for empty graphs or single-node graphs. Returns infinity
    (`float('inf')`) for disconnected graphs.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The Wiener index (Wiener, 1947) is the sum of all pairwise shortest-path distances,
originally introduced as a topological descriptor for the boiling points of alkanes
and now the standard *compactness* metric across chemistry, drug design, and
network theory: $W(G) = \sum_{u < v} d(u, v)$ on undirected graphs, or
$\sum_{u \ne v}$ on directed ones. The tree case admits a beautiful closed form:
for every edge $e$ that splits the tree into subtrees of size $n_1(e)$ and
$n_2(e)$, that edge contributes $n_1(e) \cdot n_2(e)$ to the total, so
$W(T) = \sum_e n_1(e) \cdot n_2(e)$. Disconnected graphs return $+\infty$ because
some pairs have no finite distance. Runtime is $O(V(V + E))$ for unweighted graphs
(BFS from every source) and $O(V(V + E) \log V)$ for weighted ones (Dijkstra from
every source).

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~hyper_wiener_index`) 

:   Weigh far-apart pairs more heavily.

[](`~kirchhoff_index`) 

:   Sum resistance distances instead of path distances.

[](`~mean_distance`) 

:   The average distance instead of the sum.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

On a path of four nodes the distances are `1 + 2 + 3 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 10`; a star with the
same number of nodes is more compact:

::: {#f2270d53 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

path = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "D")])
star = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("A", "C"), ("A", "D")])

path.wiener_index(), star.wiener_index()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(10.0, 9.0)
```
:::
:::


### Boiling points

`molecules()` holds the graphs of seven small molecules, one node per atom (leaving out
the hydrogens) and one edge per bond. Butane and isobutane are the same four carbons
joined in a chain and in a branch. Wiener introduced the index in 1947 to predict the
boiling points of such molecules: the more compact isobutane has the lower index, and
it boils at -11.7 °C against butane's -0.5 °C:

::: {#0306f45a .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
molecules = ec.molecules()
butane, isobutane = molecules["butane"], molecules["isobutane"]

butane.wiener_index(), isobutane.wiener_index()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(10.0, 9.0)
```
:::
:::


::: {#b3e202ad .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
butane.disjoint_union(isobutane).plot(
    label_by="element", seed=1, height=300
)
```

